# Compute insights programmatically
price_spread = comparable_median[most_expensive] / comparable_median[least_expensive]
top3_prof = prof_share.nlargest(3)
bot3_prof = prof_share.nsmallest(3)
top3_entire = entire_home_share.nlargest(3)

# Bangkok USD median
bk_median_usd = median_price_usd.get("Bangkok", np.nan)

html += f"""
    <div class="takeaway">
      <strong>1. Among cities with USD-denominated prices, {most_expensive} commands the highest median ({fmt(comparable_median[most_expensive])}/night), {price_spread:.1f}x the median in {least_expensive} ({fmt(comparable_median[least_expensive])}).</strong> Bangkok, when converted to USD (~{fmt(bk_median_usd)}/night), falls at the lower end of the price spectrum. Price gaps reflect local purchasing power, tourism demand intensity, and regulatory environments. Paris and Sydney have no price data and cannot be compared.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <strong>2. Professional hosts dominate in {top3_prof.index[0]} ({fmt_pct(top3_prof.values[0])}), {top3_prof.index[1]} ({fmt_pct(top3_prof.values[1])}), and {top3_prof.index[2]} ({fmt_pct(top3_prof.values[2])}).</strong> These markets are heavily commercialized — new entrants face established multi-listing operators. In contrast, {bot3_prof.index[0]} ({fmt_pct(bot3_prof.values[0])}) remains fragmented, offering better conditions for independent hosts to gain visibility.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <strong>3. Entire-home dominance signals commercialization.</strong> {top3_entire.index[0]} ({fmt_pct(top3_entire.values[0])}), {top3_entire.index[1]} ({fmt_pct(top3_entire.values[1])}), and {top3_entire.index[2]} ({fmt_pct(top3_entire.values[2])}) have the highest entire-home shares. Markets where entire-home listings predominate tend to have more professional operators and face greater regulatory scrutiny — policymakers often target entire-home listings when tightening short-term rental rules.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <strong>4. {highest_rpm} leads in review activity (avg {avg_rpm[highest_rpm]:.2f} reviews/month),</strong> indicating strong and recurring guest demand. High review frequency combined with high availability suggests a mature, high-throughput market. Cities with lower review rates may represent underpenetrated markets or ones where guests book through alternative channels.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <strong>5. Minimum-night requirements reveal regulatory regimes.</strong> {median_min_nights.idxmax()} (median {median_min_nights.max():.0f} nights) and cities with median stays above 30 nights likely enforce strict short-term rental caps. {median_min_nights.idxmin()} (median {median_min_nights.min():.0f} nights) offers the most flexible booking environment. Regulatory risk is highest in cities with the most restrictive minimum-night regimes — they may further tighten rules.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <strong>6. Availability gaps signal market maturity vs. regulation.</strong> {highest_avail} averages {mean_avail[highest_avail]:.0f} available days/year, suggesting commercially-oriented listings operating near full capacity. Cities with very low average availability may indicate either strong booking occupancy or listings pulled from the market due to regulatory compliance actions.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <strong>7. Price data gaps are a significant limitation.</strong> Paris and Sydney — two of the world's top tourist destinations — have no price data at all. Bangkok's prices require currency conversion with inherent approximation error. Any pricing conclusions should be validated with additional data sources before making investment decisions.
    </div>
